 Preamble

 The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for software
 and other works, specifically designed to ensure cooperation with a user community
 when the software is run on a server.

 The licenses for most software and other practical works take away your
 freedom to share and change the works.  By contrast, the GNU General Public
 License is intended to guarantee your freedom to share and change all versions
 of a program--to make sure it remains free software for all its users.  We use
 the GNU Affero General Public License for most software; the Affero license
 was designed specifically for the case of software which is used on a web site.

 The key difference between the Affero license and the ordinary GPL is the
 following: In the GPL, there is no requirement to release the source code of
 a work solely by reason of running it on a server.  The Affero license ensures
 that source code which is used on a web site must also be made available to
 the users of that site.

 When we speak of free software, we are referring to freedom, not price.  Our
 GNU General Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you have the
 freedom to distribute copies of free software (and charge for them if you
 wish), that you receive source code or can get it if you want it, that you
 can change the software or use pieces of it in new free programs, and that
 you know you can do these things.

 Developers that use our GNU General Public Licenses protect your rights with
 two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer you this
 License which gives you legal permission to copy, distribute and/or modify
 the software.

 A "work based on the Program" means the Program or any derivative work
 under copyright law: the latter includes works containing the Program or
 a portion of it, either as microcode or as a higher-level language.  To
 "propagate" a work means to do anything with it that would make you
 directly or indirectly liable for infringement under applicable copyright
 law except executing it on a computer or modifying it for private use.  It
 does not include running it on a computer or modifying the software for
 private use.

 An interaction with a user through a computer network, with the source
 code of the system not being on the same side of the network as the user,
 is called an "interaction".  Making, using, selling, offering for sale,
 or importing the Program would each constitute a form of propagation that
 may under some circumstances invoke section 3 of the ordinary GPL.

 Source Code, for a work, means the preferred form of the work for
 making modifications to it.  For an executable work, complete source
 code means all the source code for all modules it contains, plus any
 associated interface definition files, plus the scripts used to
 control compilation and installation of the executable.  However, as a
 special exception, the source code distributed need not include anything
 that is normally distributed (in either source or binary form) on the
 same component as the executable.

 If the release of a Program or part of it to the public domain under the
 ordinary GNU General Public License or to another widely used free license
 in a manner consistent with the intent of this section, the source code
 made available need not include any element that is a natural language
 implementation detail or a system library which the Program's developer
 (or a preceding developer) has added to the work solely to make it more
 efficient or to facilitate interactions with operating facilities.

